### What was the stated primary objective of the food-related legislation (e.g., food safety, biosecurity, export standards)?
Answer: The stated primary objective of the Food Bill was to modernize New Zealand's food safety regulations in order to improve food safety and reduce foodborne illness. As Food Safety Minister Kate Wilkinson stated, "The Bill is designed to simplify 30-year-old food safety regulations and ultimately aims to reduce our high level of food-borne illness and corresponding economic cost."

Source URL: https://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/food-bill-make-food-safer-%E2%80%93-not-restrict-small-traders

### Did any official government communications, press releases, or parliamentary debates in New Zealand clarify that the legislation does not ban home gardening?
Answer: Yes. An official government press release from the New Zealand Beehive website (the government's official communications platform), issued by Food Safety Minister Kate Wilkinson, directly addressed these concerns. Minister Wilkinson explicitly stated that "This Bill won't in any way affect people's right to grow food and to then exchange, sell or trade it," and further clarified that "Food grown at home for personal or family consumption, or given away to friends is excluded from the measures in the Bill." Additionally, the Q&A section of the same release addressed the concern about community food swapping, stating: "It is an age old Kiwi tradition for people to grow food for themselves and swap their excess with friends or neighbours. The Food Bill will not prevent this tradition from continuing."

Source URL: https://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/food-bill-make-food-safer-%E2%80%93-not-restrict-small-traders

### How have horticultural organizations, gardening advocacy groups, or legal experts in New Zealand interpreted the impact of this legislation on personal gardening activities?
Answer: According to the New Zealand government's official statement, horticultural organizations and gardening advocacy groups were concerned that the Food Bill would impose costly regulations on community gardens and similar activities, but Food Safety Minister Kate Wilkinson explicitly refuted this, stating: “This Bill won’t in any way affect people’s right to grow food and to then exchange, sell or trade it. Food grown at home for personal or family consumption, or given away to friends is excluded from the measures in the Bill.”

