### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The claim that New Zealand's Food Bill bans gardening is false, originating as a debunked online hoax. Official government communications, including a [press release by Food Safety Minister Kate Wilkinson](https://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/food-bill-make-food-safer-%E2%80%93-not-restrict-small-traders), explicitly state that the bill "won't in any way affect people's right to grow food and to then exchange, sell or trade it," and that food grown at home for personal consumption is excluded from its measures. The legislation, now enacted as the Food Act 2014, was designed to modernize food safety regulations for commercial activities, not to regulate non-commercial horticulture. A [HuffPost article](https://www.huffpost.com/entry/new-zealand-gardening-ban_n_57696251e4b0a75709b7d6ac) confirms that gardening is not banned, and [Know Your Meme](https://knowyourmeme.com/memes/new-zealand-gardening-law-hoax) documents the claim as an internet hoax with fabricated enforcement examples from online forums. No credible record exists of anyone being prosecuted for maintaining a home garden under the act.